WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.768
-Our next guest is a
"New York Times" best-selling

00:00:02.768 --> 00:00:03.970
author and actor you know
from "The Office."

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.505
His new show, "The Premise,"

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:08.942
is streaming now
exclusively on FX on Hulu.

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:10.043
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:12.044
-I know you want to
make a difference,

00:00:12.044 --> 00:00:16.015
and maybe this isn't
how you imagined it,

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:19.085
but this is your chance
to be a hero.

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:21.554
This is your chance to
make a difference,

00:00:21.554 --> 00:00:23.522
a big difference,
in a man's life.

00:00:23.522 --> 00:00:25.658
Ethan...

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.429
this is your shot.

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:35.768
-And, Ethan, you are not
throwing away your shot.

00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:38.304
-Please welcome back
to the show B.J. Novak.

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:40.507
♪♪

00:00:40.507 --> 00:00:41.507
-Hey.

00:00:41.507 --> 00:00:43.209
-Hi, buddy.
-How are you?

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:45.979
-Thanks for being here.
Great to see you, too.

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:47.880
Have to wave?
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.284
-Hi, everybody. Hello.
It's great to see you.

00:00:51.284 --> 00:00:53.819
-I asked Nathan this question.

00:00:53.819 --> 00:00:56.122
And I know you did some
early stand-up days.

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:58.224
You probably played in some
pretty bad rooms,

00:00:58.224 --> 00:00:59.392
as well as far as audiences.

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:01.527
-I have definitely gotten
the kind of silence

00:01:01.527 --> 00:01:02.795
that you get
in a room like this.

00:01:02.795 --> 00:01:04.029
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:04.029 --> 00:01:06.433
-Where did you first do
stand-up in New York?

00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:08.534
-The first -- Well, the first
show I ever did was in L.A.

00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:09.769
It was the
Hollywood Youth Hostel.

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:10.803
-Okay.

00:01:10.803 --> 00:01:13.405
-It was October 10, 2001,

00:01:13.405 --> 00:01:14.774
so it wasn't a funny time.

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:16.209
-Right.

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:17.910
-And there weren't a lot of
English speakers in the crowd.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.313
-Gotcha.
-And the guy before me killed.

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:23.349
He did an impression of
Robert De Niro taking a dump.

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:24.350
-Oh, wow.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:27.286
-Which transcends language
barriers.

00:01:27.286 --> 00:01:28.954
And I should have
taken that cue.

00:01:28.954 --> 00:01:31.123
And my very
dry, ironic one-liners

00:01:31.123 --> 00:01:35.695
about the tragedy that had just
happened did not -- did not

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:38.097
play as I hoped.

00:01:38.097 --> 00:01:40.633
And the emcee said sort of the
most crushing thing

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:41.767
to hear after your set.

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:45.538
He said, "Takes a lot of courage
to get up here."

00:01:47.073 --> 00:01:48.341
Yeah.

00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:51.777
So, yeah, sometimes,
the compliments are more --

00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:54.113
-More cutting than,
yeah, the zingers.

00:01:54.113 --> 00:01:56.682
Your face is the poster
for your new show.

00:01:56.682 --> 00:01:58.016
-Which is also kind of
your face.

00:01:58.016 --> 00:01:59.118
-It is.
-Yeah.

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:01.020
-And I'm not going to show a
picture...

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.354
-Thank you.

00:02:02.354 --> 00:02:04.724
-...of your show, but I'm
pretty certain we almost have

00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:07.560
the exact same pose that I had
when I first did this show.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.195
Like, I think, like,
just some version --

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:11.230
-Where's -- which camera do I --
-Yeah, they're right there.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:12.632
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:16.769
-Yeah, it's
a very obnoxious look,

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:18.371
but it's the only way, I guess,

00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:19.706
to convey,
"This is kind of a comedy."

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:20.973
-Yep.

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:22.942
-Because just me like that,
you know, it's dramatic,

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:24.810
and...is kind of not it.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.979
But it's not --
Yeah, I don't love it.

00:02:26.979 --> 00:02:30.216
-It's -- Did you -- Were you
worried and have you seen yet --

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:32.552
Being in the city,
has it been defaced yet?

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:34.420
-I'm dying for it be defaced.
-Yeah.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:35.988
-I have not -- That's when
you know you've made it,

00:02:35.988 --> 00:02:37.089
when your billboard is defaced.

00:02:37.089 --> 00:02:38.891
And I look forward
to it being defaced,

00:02:38.891 --> 00:02:41.327
but it's just --
It's been left alone.

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:42.595
-Oh, that's -- Yeah.

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:47.800
-But my plan is to, someday,
star in something

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.304
that is just really an
experiment to be defaced.

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:53.572
I have this vision of making
a movie called

00:02:53.572 --> 00:02:59.245
"The Invisible Trumpet," and the
poster is me just like this.

00:03:02.381 --> 00:03:03.850
-Like, can they resist?

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:05.017
-Yeah, no, it would really be --

00:03:05.017 --> 00:03:07.987
I really envision it as
an art contest.

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:09.055
-Yeah.
-You know what I mean?

00:03:09.055 --> 00:03:11.591
You would see
what people came up with.

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:14.861
-I wonder if, for a certain
level of graffiti artists,

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:16.930
they might think, "Too easy.

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.998
I'm not gonna even do this."

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:22.335
-I mean, yeah, it's sort of
"The New Yorker" caption contest

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:26.505
for, you know,
the defacer community.

00:03:26.505 --> 00:03:27.807
Right, 'cause what
would they draw?

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:30.810
Maybe someone would break --
break your heart with

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:31.844
what's in my mouth.

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:33.112
-With some
beautiful piece of art.

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:34.914
-Yeah, or make a statement.

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:37.583
-So, this is an anthology
series -- five episodes.

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:39.886
-Yep.
-It's great.

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:43.422
What I love about it is that
they are like short films.

00:03:43.422 --> 00:03:44.723
And I think --

00:03:44.723 --> 00:03:47.893
-Well, I would say that
they're like short movies,

00:03:47.893 --> 00:03:50.596
because short films suck.

00:03:50.596 --> 00:03:52.031
-That's a really --
I like what you're doing here.

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:53.365
I am fully on board with this.

00:03:53.365 --> 00:03:55.634
-I think short films --
and I'm sure there are some

00:03:55.634 --> 00:03:57.436
beautiful ones out there --
but short films

00:03:57.436 --> 00:04:01.740
tend to be elegant meditations
on loss or something like that,

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.343
whereas a short movie --
Movies are too long,

00:04:04.343 --> 00:04:05.377
in my opinion.

00:04:05.377 --> 00:04:06.446
Every movie is too long.

00:04:06.446 --> 00:04:08.481
They're like
2 hours and 15 minutes,

00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:10.183
and when you check your phone,

00:04:10.183 --> 00:04:13.785
it's really like six hours in a
given evening to watch a movie.

00:04:13.785 --> 00:04:16.255
And a TV show -- you think of
a TV show as shorter,

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:19.191
but it's even longer, because
you're like 400 episodes behind

00:04:19.191 --> 00:04:20.693
everyone if you start the show.

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:23.195
So, to me, a short movie
is really like,

00:04:23.195 --> 00:04:26.065
what if you had, like,
a story that had

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:28.568
some comedy and some drama and
was about something interesting

00:04:28.568 --> 00:04:31.904
and good actors and you just
made it in half an hour?

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:34.606
And you could watch
an intense little movie,

00:04:34.606 --> 00:04:35.708
and then the next one.

00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:39.445
So that's why
I wanted to do this show.

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:41.180
-I will say, with all sincerity,

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.449
I watched an episode -- because
it was recommended to me.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:44.450
One of the episodes
is called "Butt Plug."

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:45.451
-Yeah.

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:48.554
-And it is
incredibly dramatic...

00:04:48.554 --> 00:04:51.190
-Yep.
-...and also very funny.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:53.659
But I -- One of
the things I noted is that

00:04:53.659 --> 00:04:55.828
when it was over, I could not
remember the last time

00:04:55.828 --> 00:04:57.797
I had sort of,
like, uninterrupted,

00:04:57.797 --> 00:05:00.132
watched half an hour
without being distracted,

00:05:00.132 --> 00:05:01.768
because it did have, like,
the arc of a film...

00:05:01.768 --> 00:05:02.769
-Yeah.

00:05:02.769 --> 00:05:04.169
-...which was really cool --

00:05:04.169 --> 00:05:06.138
and the stakes of a film --
and, yet, at the same time,

00:05:06.138 --> 00:05:08.741
I did not feel like a huge chunk
had been out of my day.

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:09.908
-That's exactly
what I'm going for.

00:05:09.908 --> 00:05:11.944
And I do love shows
like "Black Mirror"

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:14.413
and "The Twilight Zone,"
but I hadn't --

00:05:14.413 --> 00:05:15.614
Those aren't my voice.

00:05:15.614 --> 00:05:17.683
My voice is this
very particular brand of comedy

00:05:17.683 --> 00:05:20.552
that you find funny
or you don't.

00:05:20.552 --> 00:05:22.020
Or you find dramatic
or you don't.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.590
But it's --
You know, the whole goal is,

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:27.760
what are these
intense movies that, like,

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.428
aren't weighed down with some,

00:05:29.428 --> 00:05:33.099
you know, subplot that kind of
bloats it into 90 minutes?

00:05:33.099 --> 00:05:35.067
I like movies,
too, but too long.

00:05:35.067 --> 00:05:36.569
-I mean, they're
definitely not going to --

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:38.804
I think the movie community
is not going to think

00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:41.140
this was a glowing review
by either of us.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:42.742
-No. Or the
short-film community.

00:05:42.742 --> 00:05:43.942
-Right. Now they're going to
come after us hard.

00:05:43.942 --> 00:05:45.778
-Yeah, forget Sundance
for me now.

00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:47.080
-You have a great cast.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:48.747
We saw
Ben Platt, Tracee Ellis Ross.

00:05:48.747 --> 00:05:52.718
But a really cool thing --
you got to work with Ed Asner

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:54.687
on maybe his last project

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:55.754
that we're going to
get to see him in.

00:05:55.754 --> 00:05:57.089
-Not my fault.

00:05:57.089 --> 00:06:01.493
He was really wonderful
and inspiring to work with.

00:06:01.493 --> 00:06:03.696
He was, you know, 90 years old.

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:07.066
And he would rest between takes,
which we asked a lot of him.

00:06:07.066 --> 00:06:08.768
It was quite a role,
but he wanted to do it.

00:06:08.768 --> 00:06:11.203
But he would wake up --
And, you know,

00:06:11.203 --> 00:06:13.106
his assistant would gently say,
"It's time" and wake him up.

00:06:13.106 --> 00:06:15.274
And we'd feel bad
bringing him back to set,

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:17.142
but once he started,
he would not stop.

00:06:17.142 --> 00:06:20.112
He would do improv after improv
and take after take.

00:06:20.112 --> 00:06:22.815
And it just shows you that
someone of that level

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:25.784
doing it that long,
you just have to love it.

00:06:25.784 --> 00:06:27.519
It's only love
that can make you do that.

00:06:27.519 --> 00:06:28.520
-It's a really cool thing.

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:31.090
Finally, "The Office,"

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:32.324
maybe more popular now
than it's ever been.

00:06:32.324 --> 00:06:33.526
-I know. Wow.

00:06:33.526 --> 00:06:35.428
-You Instagrammed this
the other day and said --

00:06:35.428 --> 00:06:36.829
What was your phrasing?

00:06:36.829 --> 00:06:38.130
"If you know..."

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:42.868
-Yeah, that sentence, IYKYK --
"If you know, you know."

00:06:42.868 --> 00:06:44.870
I always have to,
like, sound it out in my head.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:47.406
-This was a drink
that someone sent over to you.

00:06:47.406 --> 00:06:49.308
-Yes, this is,
I think, a 7 and 7

00:06:49.308 --> 00:06:52.211
with seven maraschino cherries
and sugar on the rim --

00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:53.846
blend it, if you can --

00:06:53.846 --> 00:06:56.015
which was what
my character ordered for

00:06:56.015 --> 00:06:58.384
Mindy Kaling's character
on an "Office" episode

00:06:58.384 --> 00:07:01.820
that I forgot long ago, because
it was like 15 years ago,

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.389
but it's fresh in
many people's minds.

00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:05.057
And this was just last night.

00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:06.259
-That is a very cool thing,

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:08.961
the long tale of love
for that show.

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:12.398
-Yeah, but the drink
I don't recommend.

00:07:12.398 --> 00:07:14.199
-New episodes of "The Premise"

00:07:14.199 --> 00:07:16.369
are available Thursdays
exclusively on FX on Hulu.

